{"kind":"artifact","text":"cmd/gs/deadline.go at exact 7ce77670f3f0a2f750863dd7d8a021823da24b9c on request/receipt-deadline, under promise git:sha1…b3f7aea on request git:sha1…469f7f2\n\nRequest d51041b3: a merge receipt must not time out on a healthy resident. Two commits over main 5876d211.\n\nThe fix is a deadline change, not a fold change.\n- After a merge has moved its target, recordMergeSuccession raises each submission's deadline to mergeSuccessionDeadline (10 minutes), on both fresh and resumed merges. A longer --deadline still wins.\n- Before the move the ordinary deadline applies, so a refused merge still fails fast.\n- The resident now clears the response write deadline on /v0/submit and the browser's /v0/act, as it already did on /v0/status. Without that, a submission over 40 s lands but the client sees EOF and reports a refusal.\n- If an answer is lost after the move (a timeout, a closed connection or a reply cut short), the merge now says the target already carries the merge, the act may have landed, and running the same gs merge or gs land again resumes from the receipt sealed in Git.\n\nConditions:\n1. Resident time on the frozen artroom copy (depth 43,744, cold resident, load 9–27). At main, the receipt submit was cancelled at 13.4 s while the resident was still reading the log; the receipt never landed. With this fix on main, the receipt took 60.3 s and landed: 8.2 s to read and check the log, 44.3 s to fold its 43,744 events, about 35 s of that in per-receipt staleness passes. The first successor took 3.8 s. With request dd845a00's fold commits underneath, the receipt takes 19.0 s and the first successor 0.40 s. The receipt is slow because it is the resident's first submission: the resident builds its own copy of the log for admission.\n2. A full gs land of the 58-successor landing on the frozen copy, at default settings with no --deadline, exited 0 and recorded all 163 acts. It was run at the pre-recut head; the recut patches are identical.\n3. The bound is stated in merge.md, land.md and serve.md: 10 minutes per submission, so a worst case of (1 + retirements + successors) × 10 minutes, and an interrupted run resumes. serve.md also says what bounds a stalled reader on the three routes: nothing on the server; a process on this machine can hold one goroutine and one fd, which is inside the trusted-process boundary.\n4. Pinned by TestMergeRecordsItsReceiptFromAResidentSlowerThanTheDeadline, TestResumedMergeRecordsItsReceiptFromAResidentSlowerThanTheDeadline, TestLandRatifiesUnderTheOrdinaryDeadlineBeforeTheMove, TestMergeSuccessionWaitsAtLeastItsOwnBound, TestMergeSaysHowToResumeWhenTheReceiptsAnswerIsLost, TestAnswerLostSeparatesALostAnswerFromAnAnswer and TestResidentHTTPServerLetsColdStatusAndAppendsOutliveWriteTimeout. Each goes red under its mutant (M1–M13).\n5. go test ./... -count=1 -timeout 40m passes at this head (36 packages).\n\nAn independent adversarial review recommended approval; its six low findings are fixed in the second commit.","body":{"commit":"7ce77670f3f0a2f750863dd7d8a021823da24b9c","path":"cmd/gs/deadline.go"}}